#build from the source

#instead of pre-compiled files (binaries), we can download source code and compile it ourselves

#however, resolving dependency issues becomes a manual task.

#source files are written in computer languages like c, c++, etc... 
#We need to compile source files into executables, which are programmes that the server can execute.

#first install a standard bundle of common compilers and similar tools. On Ubuntu it is called 
# build-essential
sudo apt install build-essential

#first check you have nmpa installed
nmap -V
#shows the version of nmap installed

#find its executable path with
which nmap

#use wget to download the desired version of nmap source using a url
wget -v https://nmap.org/dist/nmap-7.70.tar.bz2
#downloading tar ball of version 7.70 (bzip2 compression used)
#-v for verbose

#check for the tarball when finished by using ls -ltr in working dir
ls -ltr

#unzip and store files in a new directory
tar -jxvf nmap-7.70.tar.bz2
#use ls -ltr to confirm extraction into directory (directory has d in beginning)

#cd into the nmpa-7.70 dir

#find the install instruction file and use less to view it
#for nmap we need to use ./configure make and sudo make install to compile the code and make
#it available system wide

#./configure is a script that checks the server (ie to see whether its ARM or intel based, 
#32bit or 64, which compiler you have etc). can also take params to tailor the compilation of the
# software

#make compiles the software, typically using the gnu compiler gcc.

#make install takes the compiled files, and installs them plus documentation to
#your system and in some cases will setup services and schedule tasks etc.

#this is unlikely to overwrite the exisiting nmap you already have, it is likely this version would
#be installed somewhere else

#/bin is for key parts of the operating system, /usr/bin for less critical utilities and 
#/usr/local/bin for software you've chosen to manually install yourself. 
#when you type a command, the first match in the path environment of all the directories in there will
#start. So if /bin/nmap exists, it will run instead of /usr/local/bin/nmap. 
#listing the full path version will make that version run instead

#the locate command is a fast way tos earch for files, but because we just added the files, we first 
#need to manually update the index of files
sudo updatedb
#now search
locate bin/nmap

#this should find the old and new copy of nmap

#try now
/usr/bin/nmap -V
/usr/local/bin -V

#because it is installed outside of apt, it wont get updates when apt update is run

#this is not a big issue for nmap, but it would be for anything that runs as an exposed service
#You know need to track security alerts for the application and install fixed versions when they're 
#available. 
